WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.567
Structured logging records events as consistent fields rather than only
free-text sentences.

2
00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:15.465
A useful event may include an event name, timestamp, severity, component,
environment, request ID, correlation ID, outcome, reason code, and safe
contextual metadata.

3
00:00:15.465 --> 00:00:27.425
In Python, the exact logging library or observability platform may change,
but the design principle remains stable: predictable fields make logs
easier to search, alert on, review, protect, and automate.

4
00:00:27.425 --> 00:00:32.236
Event names should describe what happened at a level that supports
investigation.

5
00:00:32.236 --> 00:00:45.295
For example, an authentication failure, permission denial, export started,
export completed, admin setting changed, background job failed, dependency
timeout, or rate limit triggered event each tells a different story.

6
00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:49.351
A generic message such as something went wrong provides little value.

7
00:00:49.351 --> 00:00:54.781
A structured event can preserve meaning without copying sensitive payloads
into the record.

8
00:00:54.781 --> 00:00:57.186
Identifiers should be chosen carefully.

9
00:00:57.186 --> 00:01:06.053
Request IDs and correlation IDs help connect logs across services and
user-facing errors without exposing internal stack traces or sensitive
details.

10
00:01:06.053 --> 00:01:14.095
User, account, or tenant identifiers may be appropriate in some logs, but
teams should choose forms that respect privacy and access needs.

11
00:01:14.095 --> 00:01:18.356
Sometimes an internal stable identifier is better than an email address.

12
00:01:18.356 --> 00:01:24.748
Sometimes even a tenant identifier should be limited to audit or security
logs with stricter access controls.

13
00:01:24.748 --> 00:01:31.416
Free text still has a place, especially for human-readable context, but it
should not be the only source of meaning.

14
00:01:31.416 --> 00:01:37.052
Free-text-only logs are harder to parse, harder to redact reliably, and
easier to misinterpret.

15
00:01:37.052 --> 00:01:43.238
Structured event design turns logs into operational records rather than a
stream of improvised messages.

16
00:01:43.238 --> 00:01:49.080
That consistency is what makes monitoring, access control, retention, and
review more manageable.
